WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.664
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:09.594
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.894 --> 00:00:15.878
Unit ten: Science News. Today you will use hedging language: may, could, suggests.

4
00:00:16.178 --> 00:00:18.738
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.747
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.583 --> 00:00:24.619
Discovery.

7
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:28.336
Discovery means something found for the first time.

8
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.089
The discovery changed medicine.

9
00:00:31.683 --> 00:00:34.475
Your turn. Say: Discovery.

10
00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:38.474
Discovery.

11
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:40.531
Peer-reviewed study.

12
00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:46.117
Peer-reviewed study means research checked by other experts before publication.

13
00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:49.076
A peer-reviewed study is more reliable.

14
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:53.021
Your turn. Say: Peer-reviewed study.

15
00:00:55.867 --> 00:00:57.239
Peer-reviewed study.

16
00:00:57.983 --> 00:00:59.182
Research team.

17
00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:03.843
Research team means a group of scientists working on one project.

18
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.251
The research team studied coral reefs.

19
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.920
Your turn. Say: Research team.

20
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:14.965
Research team.

21
00:01:15.717 --> 00:01:16.613
Breakthrough.

22
00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:20.557
Breakthrough means an important new discovery or method.

23
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.242
The breakthrough could save many lives.

24
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.317
Your turn. Say: Breakthrough.

25
00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:30.272
Breakthrough.

26
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:32.145
Clinical trial.

27
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.647
Clinical trial means a test of a medicine on volunteers.

28
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.609
The clinical trial lasted two years.

29
00:01:40.217 --> 00:01:42.834
Your turn. Say: Clinical trial.

30
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.003
Clinical trial.

31
00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:48.763
Data set.

32
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:53.352
Data set means a collection of facts or numbers used in research.

33
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.878
The data set included ten thousand patients.

34
00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:59.973
Your turn. Say: Data set.

35
00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:03.881
Data set.

36
00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:06.326
Scientific consensus.

37
00:02:07.317 --> 00:02:11.046
Scientific consensus means agreement among most experts.

38
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.532
There is a scientific consensus on climate change.

39
00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:18.663
Your turn. Say: Scientific consensus.

40
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.546
Scientific consensus.

41
00:02:24.283 --> 00:02:25.377
Publication.

42
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:29.370
Publication means the printing of a study in a journal.

43
00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.529
Publication usually follows careful review.

44
00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:35.723
Your turn. Say: Publication.

45
00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:39.719
Publication.

46
00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:43.940
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.783 --> 00:02:51.769
Visal, one headline says 'Coffee cures cancer'. Another says 'Coffee may reduce cancer risk'. Which is better?

48
00:02:52.883 --> 00:03:02.548
The second. Scientists use hedging words such as may, could, might and suggests because one study rarely proves anything.

49
00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.684
So reporters should copy the hedging?

50
00:03:06.783 --> 00:03:15.754
Yes. 'The study suggests a link' is accurate. 'The study proves' is usually not, unless there is a scientific consensus.

51
00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:18.420
What makes a study strong?

52
00:03:19.517 --> 00:03:27.704
A large data set, a clinical trial, peer review before publication, and results repeated by another research team.

53
00:03:28.867 --> 00:03:30.576
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.417 --> 00:03:35.030
Grammar focus: Hedging with may and could and suggests.

55
00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:36.714
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:40.008
The new drug may reduce the risk of infection.

57
00:03:40.917 --> 00:03:43.799
The study suggests a link between sleep and memory.

58
00:03:44.683 --> 00:03:47.136
This discovery could lead to a breakthrough.

59
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:54.769
Hedge new findings with may, could, suggests or appears to. Save 'proves' for results confirmed by many studies.

60
00:03:55.517 --> 00:03:59.237
Be careful! Don't say: The study proves a link, maybe.

61
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.699
Say: The study suggests a link.

62
00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:07.450
Use hedging words, not 'proves', for new findings.

63
00:04:08.233 --> 00:04:11.176
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:13.873
Reporting Science Carefully.

65
00:04:14.173 --> 00:04:18.309
A new discovery is exciting, but a good science reporter stays careful.

66
00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:24.167
A peer-reviewed study from one research team suggests that a plant extract may protect the heart.

67
00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:30.243
The clinical trial used a large data set, yet the authors say more tests are needed.

68
00:04:31.233 --> 00:04:37.447
Because publication of one result is not scientific consensus, the headline says 'could' instead of 'will'.

69
00:04:38.433 --> 00:04:40.955
Careful words help readers trust science.

70
00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:46.161
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:47.017 --> 00:04:49.910
The study blank a link between sleep and memory.

72
00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:54.247
A, suggests. B, proving. C, is prove.

73
00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.718
The answer is A: suggests.

74
00:05:00.883 --> 00:05:04.206
Agreement among most experts is a scientific blank.

75
00:05:04.506 --> 00:05:08.322
A, consensus. B, data set. C, premiere.

76
00:05:11.867 --> 00:05:14.447
The answer is A: consensus.

77
00:05:15.583 --> 00:05:18.488
A test of a medicine on volunteers is a blank.

78
00:05:18.788 --> 00:05:23.011
A, clinical trial. B, publication. C, headline.

79
00:05:26.567 --> 00:05:28.891
The answer is A: clinical trial.

80
00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.713
Choose the most careful headline.

81
00:05:32.013 --> 00:05:37.489
A, Tea cures flu. B, Tea may help fight flu. C, Tea will stop flu.

82
00:05:41.033 --> 00:05:44.298
The answer is B: Tea may help fight flu.

83
00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.483
According to the reading, the authors say more blank are needed.

84
00:05:48.783 --> 00:05:52.994
A, tests. B, reporters. C, headlines.

85
00:05:56.533 --> 00:05:58.603
The answer is A: tests.

86
00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.731
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:03.583 --> 00:06:07.824
Why should a single new study be reported carefully rather than as final proof?

88
00:06:16.617 --> 00:06:18.233
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:18.533 --> 00:06:42.739
A single study can be wrong or limited. It may use a small group, or the result may not be repeated by other scientists. If a newspaper calls it final proof, readers may change their health choices too early. Therefore, reporters should say that the study suggests something and explain that more research is needed.

90
00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:45.015
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:45.917 --> 00:06:50.578
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and hedging with may and could and suggests.

92
00:06:50.878 --> 00:06:56.548
Today's value is scientific literacy. Understand how evidence and testing build knowledge.

93
00:06:56.848 --> 00:06:59.570
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:02.627
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

